{
	"nodes": [
		{
			"id": "header",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## Neuroendocrinology and Neuroimmunology\n\nDomain hub: hormonal and immune signalling between body and brain, including stress axes, autonomic control, circadian timing and neuroimmune interactions.\n\n[[Neuroendocrinology and Neuroimmunology Map|Open the domain map]] - snapshot of 2026-09-27; the map's *In this folder* section is the live view.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 2260,
			"height": 170,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[HPA axis]]**\n*process* - 8 sources\n\nThe hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis as a control loop: releasing factors, circulating hormones, feedback, and the timescales involved.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Stress response and the HPA axis]]**\n*mechanism* - 5 sources\n\nHPA axis anatomy and feedback, the contested role of glucocorticoids, the acute-to-chronic transition, and why single cortisol measures mislead.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 622,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Thyroid axis and mood]]**\n*process* - 5 sources\n\nThe hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis as a feedback loop, how thyroid dysfunction relates to depression, lithium's effects on thyroid function, and the mixed research record on thyroid hormone in bipolar depression.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 884,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Sex hormones and mood across the lifespan]]**\n*process* - 4 sources\n\nHow reproductive hormone transitions - the menstrual cycle, pregnancy and the postpartum period, perimenopause - relate to mood, including postpartum relapse in bipolar disorder and hormonal-fluctuation research in ADHD.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 1194,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 274,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "g0",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Axes and physiology",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 1198
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Autonomic regulation]]**\n*mechanism* - 6 sources\n\nAutonomic divisions and central control, the limits of the fight-or-flight shorthand, and why heart rate variability is not one quantity.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Autonomic measurement and heart rate variability]]**\n*method* - 6 sources\n\nHow autonomic function is measured, why heart rate variability has several definitions, and what physiological inferences are defensible.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 622,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "g1",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Autonomic",
			"x": 460,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 578
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Circadian clock biology]]**\n*process* - 6 sources\n\nThe molecular clock, its coordination across tissues, and the outputs that connect biological timing to physiology and behaviour.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Circadian rhythms and sleep]]**\n*mechanism* - 6 sources\n\nCircadian clock biology, distributed tissue clocks, sleep pressure versus timing, and the limited evidence linking rhythm disruption to mood episodes.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 622,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Sleep and circadian disruption across conditions]]**\n*framework* - 18 sources\n\nHow sleep and circadian disturbance shows up in bipolar I, ADHD, autism and PTSD/CPTSD, and what recent evidence says about cause, consequence and shared vulnerability.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 884,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "g2",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Timing",
			"x": 920,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 864
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Neuroimmune interactions and microglia]]**\n*mechanism* - 7 sources\n\nMicroglia as the brain's resident immune cells, their surveillance and responses, and how immune signalling interacts with neural function.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Cytokines and inflammation in psychiatric conditions]]**\n*theory* - 8 sources\n\nInflammatory signalling as a proposed contributor in a subset of cases, with the population-level evidence and its interpretation limits.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 646,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Blood-brain barrier]]**\n*structure* - 4 sources\n\nThe components that separate blood from neural tissue, how selectivity is achieved, and why 'leaky barrier' claims need precision.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 932,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Microbiome-gut-brain axis]]**\n*theory* - 6 sources\n\nGut microbiota as a proposed actor in brain function, the communication routes involved, and the causal limits of current evidence.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 1194,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Allostasis and chronic stress]]**\n*theory* - 7 sources\n\nStability through change: the allostatic framework, its proposed consequences of repeated activation, and how it is measured.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 1456,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#8FA8FF"
		},
		{
			"id": "g3",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Immune and barrier",
			"x": 1380,
			"y": 310,
